<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/google-ads-mi-seo-mu.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">“Should we spend on ads or on SEO?” This is the question we hear most from business owners. The short answer: they don’t do the same job. The right answer to <b>Google Ads or SEO</b> depends on your site’s age, your budget and how quickly you need customers.</p>
      <p>In this article we put the two channels side by side: how they work, where the cost comes from, when each one comes out ahead, and how AI overviews affected both in 2025–2026. At the end you’ll find a sample budget approach for three different business scenarios.</p>
      <p>If you are new to SEO, we recommend first reading our article “ <a href="seo-nedir">What is SEO</a> ”; here we focus on comparison and decision-making.</p>
      <h2 id="farki" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">The key difference between Google Ads and SEO</h2>
      <p>When you search on Google, you see two kinds of listings on the results page. Results labelled “Sponsored” are <b>Google Ads</b> ads; the advertiser pays for each click. The unlabelled results below or between them are <b>organic</b> results; you can’t pay to get in there, you earn it through SEO.</p>
      <p>Google strictly separates the two: advertising doesn’t boost your organic ranking, and stopping ads doesn’t lower it. The two channels compete on the same page, but their rules are completely different.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Google Ads</th><th>SEO</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Speed of results</b></td><td>Once the campaign is approved, usually the same day</td><td>Weeks to months, depending on competition</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Cost model</b></td><td>Pay per click + management effort</td><td>Content, technical work and time; clicks are free</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Durability</b></td><td>Visibility stops when the budget runs out</td><td>Earned visibility accumulates and lasts for a while</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Control</b></td><td>Keywords, region, time, device and copy are fully up to you</td><td>Google decides rankings; indirect control</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Measurement</b></td><td>Cost per click and per conversion are clearly visible</td><td>Impact spreads over a longer time, harder to measure</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Best suited for</b></td><td>Quick demand, campaigns, testing, seasonal sales</td><td>Long-term, sustainable, low unit-cost traffic</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h2 id="ads-nasil" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">How does Google Ads work, and what determines the cost?</h2>
      <p>Google Ads is an auction system, but the highest bidder doesn’t always win. According to the Google Ads help pages, whether an ad is shown and in which position is determined by <b>Ad Rank</b>. The main factors that make up Ad Rank:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Your bid:</b> The maximum amount you are willing to pay for a click. What you actually pay is usually less.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Ad and landing page quality:</b> How relevant the ad is to the search, the expected click-through rate, and how useful and usable the landing page is. Quality Score sums this up.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Search context:</b> The search term, the user’s location, device, time of search and the other results on the page.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Assets and thresholds:</b> The expected impact of ad assets such as phone numbers and sitelinks, and the minimum quality thresholds required for each position.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>This system has an important consequence: in Google’s own words, high-quality ads lead to lower cost per click. In other words, a business advertising to a poor landing page pays more for the same click than its competitor. How efficiently your ad budget works depends largely on the quality of your website.</p>
      <p>Cost per click varies widely by industry, city, competition and season; in fields such as law, insurance and aesthetics a single click can be quite expensive, while niche local searches can stay much more affordable. You can see current estimates for your industry in Google Ads’ free Keyword Planner tool.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Common mistake:</b> Sending all ad traffic to the homepage. Linking each ad group to a separate landing page that directly describes the searched service and leads to a single action increases both conversions and Quality Score. We explain how to do this in <a href="landing-page-rehberi">our landing page guide</a>.</div></div>
      <h2 id="seo-maliyet" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">Where does the cost of SEO come from?</h2>
      <p>“SEO is free” is half true. You don’t pay Google for organic clicks; but earning visibility takes effort, and that effort has a price:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Technical foundation:</b> A fast, mobile-friendly, crawlable site. You’ll find the details in <a href="teknik-seo-kontrol-listesi">our technical SEO checklist</a>.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Content:</b> Rich pages for each service and guide articles that answer customers’ questions. This is usually the most time-consuming item.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Authority:</b> Natural links, industry directories, press mentions and, for local businesses, reviews.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Time:</b> The time you wait for results is also a cost; during that period you need to find customers through other channels.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>SEO’s economic advantage emerges over time. A service page that ranks well keeps bringing visitors every month without extra click fees. With ads, your hundredth customer costs as much as your first, or even more as competition grows.</p>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>Ads are a rented shop window; SEO is your own property. Both have their place, but you don’t want to pay rent forever.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="ne-zaman-ads" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">When does Google Ads make more sense?</h2>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If your site is new:</b> Bringing in your first customers with ads while you don’t yet have organic visibility gives the business breathing room.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If you need quick results:</b> For a new branch opening, a seasonal product, a campaign period or an end-of-month target, there is no time to wait.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If you want to test something:</b> Within a few weeks you can see whether there is demand for a new service and which message gets more clicks. This data later guides your SEO content too.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If organic competitors are very strong:</b> In searches dominated by big brands and marketplaces, ads may be the only way to be visible in the short term.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>For emergency services:</b> For jobs people search for “right now”, such as locksmiths, plumbers and tow trucks, call ads bring phone calls directly.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="ne-zaman-seo" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">When does SEO make more sense?</h2>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If you are thinking long term:</b> If your business will offer the same services for years, organic visibility becomes the lowest unit-cost channel over time.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If your customers research before deciding:</b> In fields such as software, corporate services, education and health tourism, customers read and compare before buying. Showing up with guide content on that journey builds trust.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If click costs are very high in your industry:</b> Where every click is expensive, organic traffic is worth correspondingly more.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If you are a local business:</b> Visibility in Google Business Profile and on maps can bring steady calls and visits without ads. We cover this in detail in <a href="yerel-seo-google-isletme-profili">our local SEO guide</a>.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>If your budget can’t sustain regular ads:</b> Small, intermittent ad budgets can’t compete in the auction and may be wasted. In that case, investing effort in lasting assets, namely your site and content, can be more efficient.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="yapay-zeka-etkisi" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">How are AI overviews affecting both channels?</h2>
      <p>Google’s AI Overviews and AI Mode features were gradually rolled out in Türkiye from February 2026. This change affects the two channels differently.</p>
      <h3>On the organic side</h3>
      <p>For simple informational queries, clicks may decline because part of the answer is summarised on the results page. In Pew Research Center’s 2025 study in the US, users clicked on a traditional result in 8 percent of visits when an AI summary was shown, compared with 15 percent when there was none. On the other hand, Google says clicks from links in AI Overviews tend to be higher quality, with users spending more time on the site. There is no special technical requirement to be cited as a source in these overviews; indexed, helpful and original content is enough.</p>
      <h3>On the advertising side</h3>
      <p>Google is testing showing ads below and within AI answers where relevant, and is gradually expanding this. In addition, as the summary takes up more space on the results page, the number of traditional results and ads visible on the first screen may decrease. This makes being visible in the highest-intent commercial searches more important than simply “being on the first page”.</p>
      <p>Practical takeaway: accept some traffic loss on informational searches but keep producing content for brand awareness; on high purchase-intent searches, be strong in both organic and ads. Someone who has decided to buy won’t settle for a summary; they will click to get a quote.</p>
      <h2 id="birlikte" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">The best results: using Google Ads and SEO together</h2>
      <p>For most businesses, the right answer isn’t “one or the other” but “how much of each, at which stage”. The two channels feed each other:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>You learn from ad data which keywords actually drive sales, and plan your SEO content around them.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>You reduce ad spend on keywords where you already rank highly organically and shift it to keywords where you are organically weak.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Appearing in both ads and organic results puts you on the same results page twice, building trust.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>A fast, well-designed site improves the efficiency of both: Quality Score in ads, rankings and conversion in organic.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Three scenarios, three approaches</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Recommended weighting</th><th>Why?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Newly founded business, new site</b></td><td>Ads-heavy at first, with an SEO foundation in parallel</td><td>First customers come from ads; the site and content mature in the meantime</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Local business a few years old</b></td><td>Mostly local SEO and Business Profile, ads during peak season</td><td>Maps and reviews bring steady demand; ads support busy periods</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Growing company in a competitive industry</b></td><td>Balanced between both, adjusted based on data</td><td>Ads protect market share; SEO lowers unit cost over time</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>These ratios aren’t hard rules; they are starting points. To see how many customers each channel actually brings, setting up conversion tracking (forms, phone, WhatsApp, sales) from the start is essential. You can’t optimise a budget you don’t measure.</p>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// CHECKLIST BEFORE DECIDING YOUR BUDGET</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is it clear how quickly you need customers?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have you checked the estimated cost per click in your industry?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does ad traffic go to a relevant landing page rather than the homepage?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are form, phone and WhatsApp conversions tracked?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Do you know the keywords where you are already visible organically?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is the site fast and easy to use on mobile?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Which is better, Google Ads or SEO?</summary><p>Which is better depends on the business’s situation. Google Ads delivers quick results and is ideal for testing, but traffic stops when the budget runs out. SEO starts more slowly, but earned visibility accumulates and lowers unit costs in the long run. For most businesses, using both together in stages is the most efficient approach.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Does advertising on Google Ads affect my SEO rankings?</summary><p>No. Google keeps ads and organic results separate; advertising doesn’t raise organic rankings, and stopping ads doesn’t lower them. However, learning from ad data which keywords drive sales can indirectly strengthen your SEO strategy.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How much budget do I need for Google Ads?</summary><p>There is no fixed figure; the budget depends on the cost per click in your industry, the target region and the number of customers you expect. Google Ads’ free Keyword Planner tool shows estimated costs. Starting with a small test budget and increasing it based on conversion data is usually the safest approach.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Should I stop advertising when I start SEO?</summary><p>Usually not. Since SEO results take time, continuing ads during this period keeps customers flowing. Gradually reducing ad spend on keywords where you reach the top organically and shifting it to areas where you are organically weak makes for a healthier transition.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How is AI search affecting ads and SEO?</summary><p>AI Overviews and AI Mode can reduce organic clicks on simple informational queries, while Google is gradually expanding ads within and below AI answers. Being visible in both organic and ads on high purchase-intent searches, and producing original content, is more important than ever.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>Google Ads and SEO aren’t rivals; they are two tools working on different time horizons: ads carry today, SEO carries tomorrow. The right decision depends on where your business stands today and where it wants to be in the coming months; and the common factor that determines the efficiency of both is a fast, clear, conversion-focused website.</p>
      <p>At CYF Tech we develop <a href="web-cozumleri">websites and landing pages</a> where ads and organic traffic work most efficiently. If you would like to assess together how ready your site is for each channel, <a href="iletisim">write to us</a>.</p>
